INT. HARROWGATE MUSEUM OF NATURAL CURIOSITIES - TAXIDERMY HALL - NIGHT

Glass cases in rows, and in every case a creature caught in the middle of a thought: a fox mid-pounce, an owl with one wing half raised, a stoat frozen beside a nest of china eggs. The gaslamps hiss. The floor smells of beeswax and arsenic.

PROFESSOR ALDOUS BLYTHE, 60s, in a velvet jacket and a monocle, strolls along the cases with a candle, humming, while ROSALIND PEMBERTON-SHAW, 30s, the junior curator, trails him with a notebook, writing and sneezing, and CONSTABLE HARGROVE, 40s, a heavy man in a cape, stands with his hands behind his back, studying a stuffed heron as though it has insulted him.

BLYTHE
You see, Constable, the trick of the thing is not the skin. It is the eyes.

HARGROVE
Glass, ain't they?

BLYTHE
Glass, certainly. But placed by a hand that knew the animal.

ROSALIND
Professor, with respect, the Constable is here about the missing curator.

BLYTHE
Ah, yes. Dear Mr. Fitch. He did so love the badgers.

HARGROVE
Last seen Thursday?

BLYTHE
Thursday evening. Closing time. He went down to oil the hinges on the Great Elk.

The three of them turn toward the end of the hall. The Great Elk stands in its alcove, antlers spread like a cathedral window, glass eyes catching the candle.

Something shifts behind the elk.

ARCHIBALD FITCH, 50s, in a rumpled waistcoat, steps from behind the elk, alive, pale, holding an oil can. He looks at them like a man waking from a dream.

FITCH
I fell asleep.

Silence.

ROSALIND
Mr. Fitch! We thought you were dead!

FITCH
For four days?

BLYTHE
(smoothly)
Well. It was a very long afternoon.

Hargrove frowns. He looks at the elk. At Fitch. At Blythe, whose candle has begun to shake.

HARGROVE
Professor. There's a piece of tweed in that elk's antlers.

Everyone looks up. A scrap of grey cloth. Wet, dark at one end.

BLYTHE
That is... decorative.
